    {
      "question": "Why did the authors choose self-attention over recurrent layers?",
      "expected": "complexity, parallelization, path length",
      "answer": "The authors chose self-attention over recurrent layers for the following reasons:\n\n1.  **Computational Complexity and Parallelization**: Self-attention layers have lower total computational complexity per layer and allow for more parallelization compared to recurrent layers. Specifically, a self-attention layer connects all positions with a constant number of sequentially executed operations, whereas a recurrent layer requires O(n) sequential operations [2].\n2.  **Path Length for Long-Range Dependencies**: Self-attention reduces the path length between long-range dependencies. The shorter the paths forward and backward signals have to traverse, the easier it is to learn long-range dependencies, which is a key challenge in sequence transduction tasks [2].\n3.  **Interpretability**: Self-attention can yield more interpretable models, as individual attention heads clearly learn to perform different tasks and exhibit behavior related to the syntactic and semantic structure of sentences [3].",
      "num_chunks": 5,
